#
# A domain object: a thing the code reasons about, detached from the database. A value
# without identity — a money, a date range — is one of these too, and lives in the same
# tree.
#
# **It holds a shape and validates that shape. It computes nothing.** Deriving a value is
# an operation's job, and a shape is not an operation.
#
# **It holds other shapes; it never copies their fields.** `Booking` carrying
# `supplier_name` and `supplier_email` has taken two of `Supplier`'s columns and made them
# its own, and a flattened field is the first column of the next god object.
class Shape
  include TypedArguments

  # **A shape never holds a record, and an argument is how one gets in.**
  #
  # The call graph stops a shape *naming* a record — `PersonRecord.find` in a shape is
  # refused. It cannot stop `ProfileShape.new(person: PersonRecord.find(1))`, because at the
  # shape there is no record anywhere in the source: there is a keyword called `person`, and
  # nothing a cop reads says what it holds. Every rule about shapes leaks through that one
  # argument. A shape holding a record can lazily load an association, write through it, and
  # reopen the database from the presentation layer — with `@person.orders` and no constant
  # for anything to see.
  #
  # So it is checked here, where the answer is exact: the object is in hand and it can simply
  # be asked what it is. Reaching a record smuggled in under any name, in an array, in a
  # hash, or handed to a nested shape.
  def self.new(*arguments, **keywords, &block)
    shape = super
    refuse_persistence(shape)
    shape
  end

  def self.refuse_persistence(shape)
    shape.instance_variables.each do |name|
      value = shape.instance_variable_get(name)
      next unless persistence?(value)

      raise TypeError, "#{shape.class}##{name} holds #{value.class}. A shape is a value " \
                       "detached from the database — hand it the fields it needs, or a " \
                       "shape built from them, never the record."
    end
  end

  # A collection holding one is holding one. Checked to the element because
  # `lines: person.orders` is the shape this arrives in far more often than a bare record.
  def self.persistence?(value)
    case value
    when Array then value.any? { |element| persistence?(element) }
    when Hash then value.any? { |key, element| persistence?(key) || persistence?(element) }
    else record?(value)
    end
  end

  # **Asked of the loaded constant, never of the name.** A repository can call its records
  # anything; what makes one a record is its ancestry. `defined?` because a shape is a PORO
  # and this file must load in a suite that never booted Rails.
  def self.record?(value)
    (defined?(::ActiveRecord::Base) && value.is_a?(::ActiveRecord::Base)) ||
      (defined?(::ActiveRecord::Relation) && value.is_a?(::ActiveRecord::Relation))
  end

  private_class_method :refuse_persistence, :persistence?, :record?

  # Value semantics, without a macro. Two shapes of the same class holding the same
  # values are the same shape — which is what lets one be compared, deduplicated and
  # asserted on in a test without anybody writing `==` per class.
  def ==(other)
    other.class == self.class && other.state == state
  end

  alias eql? ==

  def hash
    ([self.class] + state).hash
  end

  def inspect
    pairs = instance_variables.map { |name| "#{name.to_s.delete("@")}: #{instance_variable_get(name).inspect}" }

    "#<#{self.class.name} #{pairs.join(", ")}>"
  end

  protected

  def state
    instance_variables.sort.map { |name| instance_variable_get(name) }
  end
end
